"""
WHY MERCURY IS LIQUID — relativity melts a metal (Blender 4.2+ / 5.x)   @AlexCarten_

Mercury has 80 protons, so its inner electrons move at about Z*alpha = 80/137 = 58% of the speed of light.
Relativity pulls the outer 6s shell in, and in mercury that shell is already full (6s2): the atoms have
almost nothing to share, so they barely bond. Relativistic simulations (2017) put the melting point at
~+82 C without relativity and -39 C with it (the real value): relativity lowers it by ~105 degrees.

Every atom is a cloud of grains (hydrogen-like 6s / 5d / core shapes, sampled from |psi|^2) — an illustration.

File > New > General -> Scripting -> Open -> Run Script -> Ctrl+F12
Video: ~/OrbitalRenders/mercury/mercury.mp4   (music: mercury_music.wav, same timing — drop it in CapCut at 0)
No Python runs during playback/render (baked keyframes + geometry nodes).
"""
